WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:07.520
- For many, many people who disappeared,

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.723
were killed by the
Nazis, there is nothing.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.093
There's no grave,

00:00:12.093 --> 00:00:15.260
there's absolutely no
inscription whatsoever

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.010
except for a line,

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:21.150
birthdate sometimes,

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:22.755
and a name.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.030
These documents, it's a testimony.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.697
It's a witness to what happened.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.940
The Arolsen Archives hold
30 million documents.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.330
It's the largest world-wide archive

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.720
on the persecution by the Nazis

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.760
and on survivors after the war.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:52.260
We had the intention to publish
large collections online,

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.600
and we didn't have the resources,

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.770
so we were very privileged to find

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.860
an excellent partner with Ancestry.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.480
- The partnership essentially
involves the archives,

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.740
digitizing the millions of
paper documents that they have,

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.880
and Ancestry creating searchable indexes,

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.883
and people everywhere can
search the records for free.

00:01:13.183 --> 00:01:16.780
- Seven and a half years ago,
I found a list from Auschwitz

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.890
with my mother's signature on it.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.580
It was a list of 227 names that came

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.310
from the Arolsen Archive in Germany.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:29.280
We can see the front is
Auschwitz, November 29th, 1944.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.770
It's a list of women prisoners.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.470
This is my mother's name.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.230
This is the number that was on her arm.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.130
This is her signature, and
what's most compelling to me

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.670
are the signatures, each different,

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:42.500
each representing their personality.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.170
What I've been doing since then

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.910
is going through name by name,

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:47.820
trying to determine what
happened to these girls.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:48.670
What's their story?

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:49.520
Where'd they come from?

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:50.623
Did they survive?

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.920
I felt compelled to go to Arolsen

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.640
to see if I could learn anything more,

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.740
both about girls on the list
as well as family members.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:01.950
This was a girl, 15 years old.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.280
As I was growing up,

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.930
my mother told me if I would
have an extra piece of bread,

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.440
I would give it to Margica,

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.700
and if you had a gift of bread,

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:12.950
that means you get to live another day.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:18.180
- It is more important than
ever to show what can happen

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.410
if these values about
solidarity, about equality,

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.493
about respect are not upheld.

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:27.760
- Living memory is dying for those

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.880
that were involved in the Holocaust.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.190
We need to capture that
information so that

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.670
even when they are gone,

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:36.230
experiences that they went
through can be preserved

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.388
and shared for future generations.

00:02:40.913 --> 00:02:43.040
- What, what is this?

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.960
That's my grandfather as well.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.840
It's extraordinary to
see his actual signature

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.340
and that he touched this card.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.400
- It is a privilege for us to contribute

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.530
because of people like
Allan were researching

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:02.230
their relatives but also
other victims of the Nazis

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:04.330
because we contribute to the fact

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:05.930
that they will not be forgotten.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:08.690
- Let's see.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:11.790
This would be a picture
of my mother in Germany.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.760
I have the ability to do
this sort of research.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:14.690
I have the time.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:16.400
Many people don't.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.253
For those who can't go where
the sources are stored,

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:23.930
the ability to look at it
online is extraordinary,

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:28.100
and it's a wonderful thing that Ancestry

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.190
is making it available for free to anyone

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:32.600
who wants to see.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.620
The reason I'm doing this is not so much

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.920
to pass this to my children,

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.820
but to their great, great,
great, great grandchildren

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.700
because one day when they
wake up and say who am I?

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:45.420
What am I going through here?

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.300
And when they look back
and they see what happened

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.370
with their great, great, great grandmother

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:53.178
and her resilience, I want
them to take that lesson.

